{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuSpWru7QzRMZPfR6r4M","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The dashboard and the alert rule both exist. I didn't open the dashboard to check how the panels render, and I haven't seen the rule fire.\n\n**What the data holds:** The logs data view has a `timestamp` time field. The main fields are `response` (HTTP status, stored as a string), `bytes`, `extension`, `clientip`, `geo.src` and `geo.dest`, `machine.os`, `host`, `url` and `request`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-d1a10699 Web logs overview\" is at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-d1a10699-web-logs-overview-09817a7e5b65. It defaults to the last 7 days and has four panels:\n- Total requests, as a single number.\n- Requests over time, as a line chart.\n- Requests by response code, as a pie chart.\n- Average bytes by file extension, as a bar chart.\n\nMy first create call failed because the line chart had no time field set. I fixed that and re-ran it, and the dashboard was created on the second call.\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-d1a10699 5xx spike\" (id `68b87d0e-073f-4fdb-be0c-341b578966b1`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- It runs every minute.\n- It matches `response.keyword` against the pattern `5[0-9][0-9]`.\n- It fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within a 5-minute window.\n\nThe rule is created disabled by default, so I enabled it. It has no notification action attached, so a firing will only show up as an alert in Kibana."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":993,"cache_read_input_tokens":20385,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":993},"output_tokens":535,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0